  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Isolated thunderstorms across western Montana through late-\n   afternoon, with outflow wind gusts to 40 mph and lightning\n   being the primary storm threats. \n\n - A cold front arrives Saturday, bringing cooling temperatures,\n   gusty westerly winds, and precipitation.\n\nSatellite imagery late this morning shows an upper level wave\nmoving across eastern Washington and Oregon. Showers and \nthunderstorms have developed this morning across much of western \nMontana and Lemhi County, ID fueled by mid-level moisture and \ninstability as this upper level waves continues to march eastward.\nHigh-resolution models and forecast soundings suggest convection \nwill impact western Montana through mid-late afternoon before \nshifting east of the divide by early this evening. Southwest to \nwesterly wind gusts of 20-30 mph will develop by late-afternoon \nthrough the early evening as the trough pushes through, acting to \nenhance vertical mixing, with winds aloft mixing to the surface.  \n\nLooking ahead to Friday and the weekend, a low remains on track \nto move across the Pacific Northwest into the Northern Rockies \nby late Saturday into Sunday. Daytime high temperatures will drop\n15-20F by Sunday. Showers and thunderstorm coverage increases \nSaturday as the low moves further inland. As far as precipitation \ngoes, chances for wetting rains (more than 0.10 in) reaches \n80-90% for west-central and southwest Montana, as well as Lemhi \nCounty. Confidence for wetting rain decreases across northwest \nMontana, as probabilities for wetting rains drop below 30 percent.\n\nTemperatures rebound early next week towards seasonal normals, \nwhile ensemble solutions are indicating more unsettled weather \nlater in the week.",
